PREDICTIONS, stated before the run and not adjusted after it
  alpha 0.558 (band 0.529-0.588), bf16 at 2 bytes, ridge 155.9 Op/B, band 145.8-158.6 Op/B
  ridge source: calibration: measured on this device: NVIDIA H200 (measured), 682.1 TFLOP/s bf16 over 4374.3 GB/s (triad pattern); calibrated 2026-09-10 at c0644bea
  ridge band source: this device's own bandwidth patterns (copy, read_reduce, read_stream, triad, write), carried as a ratio against the triad ceiling
  bandwidth source: 4374.3 GB/s, calibration: measured on this device: NVIDIA H200 (measured), 4374.3 GB/s (triad pattern)
  iterations per trial: median 85 over 171 timed cells, range 17-422. Sized per cell by the instrument from --cell-budget-ms, not by --iters, which is retired as a timing knob.
  BLOCK_M   AI cap   crossing @ridge 145.8       crossing @ridge 158.6
       32     57.3   NO CROSSING EVER       NO CROSSING EVER    
          BLOCK_M=32 never crosses at alpha=0.558: its AI cap 2*BM/(alpha*b) = 57.3 Op/B sits at or below the ridge 145.8 Op/B, so no batch size makes it compute bound. This is the study's own claim arriving, not a missing number
       64    114.7   NO CROSSING EVER       NO CROSSING EVER    
          BLOCK_M=64 never crosses at alpha=0.558: its AI cap 2*BM/(alpha*b) = 114.7 Op/B sits at or below the ridge 145.8 Op/B, so no batch size makes it compute bound. This is the study's own claim arriving, not a missing number
      256    458.8   r=  145.8 T=    1167 n=1   r=  158.6 T=    1269 n=1
  every AI cap above is 2*BM/(alpha*b) from a FITTED alpha and is HIGH by ai_model.lin_overstatement = 1+phi+delta (alpha_a is unmeasured anywhere in this repo and delta is taken as 0, so each range is a bracket and a lower bound): BM=32 1.02-1.51x, BM=64 1.03-2.02x, BM=256 1.12-5.07x
  GATE 3 will test alpha > 0.33 (midpoint of 0.558 and 0.1) as an INFORMATIONAL line; the VERDICT is whether the fitted alpha's interval overlaps the band [0.529, 0.588], in both directions
  GATE 4 will test BLOCK_M=64 against the roof in fractions of ridge x bandwidth: this run's ceiling 0.736, the retracted world's 1.000, so the gate is 0.95 (reached the roof) and 0.868 (passed its own ceiling)

MEASURED  qwen2-57b-a14b bf16  165 cells, 52 of them exactly-full tile stacks
  compute plateau 367.6 TFLOP/s useful, taken as the roof every roof fraction below is against
  that plateau is 53.9% of ridge x bandwidth (682 TFLOP/s). Far below 100% means nothing in the sweep reached a roof and every roof fraction is relative to something that is not one.
  per-cell timing spread, median 5.48%
  compute reference: BLOCK_M=256 ladder, 4 treads, proportional to 2.4%: compute branch 2.4800 ms per tile, fixed cost 0.2692 ms
    LEVEL roof fraction      0.533   gate <= 1.10 of ridge x bandwidth (a compute branch cannot beat the roof)
    LEVEL non-vacuity        0.385   gate <  1.00 of one full weight read, scaled to the smallest block size (at or above, NO tread anywhere can be memory bound and every alpha is unidentifiable by construction)
      floor                 0.205   of the roof, = 2 BM_min / (b x ridge): the smallest swept tile's own AI cap at alpha=1, over the ridge
      footing            DENSE. The memory branch is compared against one full weight read at the measured pin rate, so the floor is a fraction of the dense GEMM roof. A fused layer counting only its two GEMMs' FLOPs has never exceeded 0.756 of that roof, so a floor above 0.756 cannot be met by any fused-layer reference on any card.
    LEVEL vs smaller BM      0.833   gate <= 1.25 at matched exactly-full rows, over 4 comparison(s)
  132 SMs (reported by the driver), one resident CTA per SM assumed for every wave count
  up-GEMM waves run 38.8 to 1280.0 across the sweep. The smallest is the smallest tile stack, and even it is many waves deep, so no cell here is a partial-wave measurement and occupancy cannot be the thing that moved between two adjacent cells.

THE LADDER: milliseconds per exactly-full tile stack, which is where gates 2, 3 and 4 are read
  alpha is a LOWER bound (the fused layer's fixed cost sits in the denominator); alpha-hi takes that cost out, and alpha-corrected also removes activation traffic
  alpha here is B/(A+B), which is (alpha_b+phi)/(1+phi+delta) and NOT a weight miss fraction: see moe/bench/ai_model.py and LadderFit.alpha
  6 cell(s) excluded for a DRIFTING clock: the SM clock moved while they were timed, so each median is a blend of two operating points and the time belongs to neither.
    Reached: the plateau, the compute reference, the noise estimate that sets the margin, and gates 1, 2, 3 and 4. 1 of them were aligned treads and are counted per ladder below.
  145 cell(s) KEPT with LEVEL failed HIGH and 0 KEPT with LEVEL failed LOW: their SM clock under load sat steadily outside the band around the clock the roof was measured at. On a power-capped card that band is not a health check: the under-load clock is set per tile by the kernel's own power draw (H200, 700 W: BM=128/BN=64 a median 1395 MHz, BM=256 1650, memory-shaped 1950-1980, against a 1485 MHz calibration GEMM). Their milliseconds are measurements and stay in every fit; their fraction of the FIXED roof is off by the clock ratio -- OVERSTATED on the high side, UNDERSTATED on the low -- and the roof at their own clock (the driver's roof_at_cell_clock_tflops) is the one to read them against.
    The plateau gates 1, 2 and 4 read is still the fixed-roof fraction, so on a boosted cell it is HIGH and on a sagged cell LOW: gate 4's absence claim (the null tile never reaches the roof) is biased toward FAIL by the first and toward PASS by the second. 45 high and 0 low were aligned treads and are counted per ladder below.
